# Cloudflare Pages reads this file automatically — no dashboard setting needed.
#
# Why these two lines matter more than they look:
#   sw.js is the service worker. If the browser caches it for even an hour,
#   a phone that already installed the app keeps serving yesterday's build and
#   no amount of redeploying reaches it. must-revalidate makes every open ask
#   "is this still current?" — a 304 costs nothing, a stale app costs trust.
#   index.html is the document that names the current build (v41.9). Same rule.
#
# Everything else (js/, css/, icons/) is left alone on purpose: those files are
# NOT fingerprinted, so an immutable header would pin a year-old module after
# one deploy. Default revalidation is the safe default here.

# ── Baseline security headers (added Nov 2026) ─────────────────────────────
# None of these were set before. A Content-Security-Policy is deliberately NOT
# added yet: the app uses inline <style> and inline event handlers, so a strict
# CSP would blank the screen. Add it later together with the refactor.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: SAMEORIGIN
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(self), camera=(self), microphone=(self), payment=()

/sw.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
  Service-Worker-Allowed: /

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/robots.txt
  Content-Type: text/plain; charset=utf-8

/sitemap.xml
  Content-Type: application/xml; charset=utf-8

# Digital Asset Links — read fresh every time. A cached copy silently breaks
# Trusted Web Activity verification, which shows up as a browser bar in the app.
/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: no-store
